{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Every man — his mother and his father — you shall fear.</b> Every one of you shall fear his father and his mother — this is its plain sense. And its midrash: I have only 'man'; from where do I include a woman? When it says ״תִּירָאוּ״ — here are two. If so, why is 'man' stated? Because a man has it in his own power to act, but a woman — another's authority is over her. (Sifra; Kiddushin 30)" "<b>His mother and his father — you shall fear.</b> Here he placed mother before father, because it is manifest before Him that a son fears his father more than his mother. And in honor he placed father before mother, because it is manifest before Him that a son honors his mother more than his father, because she coaxes him with words. (ibid.)" "<b>And My Sabbaths you shall keep.</b> He juxtaposed Sabbath to the fear of one's father, to say: even though I warned you about the fear of your father — if he says to you, 'Profane the Sabbath,' do not listen to him. And so with all the other commandments. (Bava Metzia 32)" "<b>I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> You and your father are both bound by My honor; therefore you shall not listen to him to nullify My words. What is fear? He does not sit in his place, and does not speak in his place, and does not contradict his words. And what is honor? He feeds and gives drink, clothes and shoes, brings in and takes out. (Kiddushin 31)"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:05.253996908Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}